    "snippet": "Thank you. You know the American public school education system faces challenges no doubt, but with 90 percent of our students enrolled, it will always be the foundation on which to build our Nation's future. We need these long-term investments, and the research to get students on track to success, but also ready for t...",
    "full_text": "Thank you. You know the American public school education system faces challenges no doubt, but with 90 percent of our students enrolled, it will always be the foundation on which to build our Nation's future. We need these long-term investments, and the research to get students on track to success, but also ready for the future. We looked at the proliferation, for example, of artificial intelligence in schools, and the workforce. That means we need to instill creative critical thinking as Representative Burgess mentioned. It is important to educate critical thinkers. Years of research shows that students who have arts education do better in other academic subjects and are more likely to stay in school. For those of you who want to see academic achievement and improvement, I invite you to join on my bipartisan Arts Education for All Bill. When we think about NAEP scores, those are test scores. Keep in mind there is a mental health crisis in our Nation's schools. A North Carolina publication recently revealed that nearly 1 in 5 adolescents in North Carolina suffered from depression, and 1 in 10 of the high school students there reported attempting suicide. Colleagues, this is a serious issue we should be grappling with as well. Also, Dr. Brumley, you said parents want their kids to be safe. Absolutely they do. After the school shooting at St. Helena High School in Greensburg, Louisiana, a high school student said my generation is being forced to normalize living and learning in constant fear of gunfire ringing out in our schools. Students who are worried about being gunned down in their classroom are in survival mode, they are not focused on getting good test scores. Students who live in fear of ICE raids, as Representative Lee mentioned, coming into their school and taking their classmates. They will have trouble focusing on learning, reading and math. Finally, Mr. Chairman, I hope we can work together to fix our crumbling school buildings, so that every child, regardless of their background or their income, can learn in a place that reinforces to them that they have a bright future. Thank you, I look forward to continuing the conversation on these important issues, and I yield back the balance of my time. Chairman Kiley. Thank you very much. I want to say thank you again to our truly outstanding witnesses. I think this was a very important hearing, and I want to also thank members on both sides for the thoughtful questions, while we might not agree on every issue, there seems to be quite a bit of agreement that the reading wars are over, that we need to return to teaching foundational literacy and foundational numeracy. I think we have three takeaways from today's hearing. No. 1 is the stakes. We heard from Ms. Roughton that two thirds of kids are not reading at proficiency right now. We also heard from Dr. Wingfield, that if you do not read at a proficient level by third grade that you are four times more likely to drop out of high school. The second takeaway is the science. Ms. Roughton said, ``We know what works, but it just has not been fully implemented across the country.'' Dr. Brumley in Louisiana is calling this a back-to-basics approach that involves building foundational literacy and numeracy skills. Dr. Harrison, in his district in Alabama, has stressed the importance of making data base decisions. I think you said that you eat, sleep and breath data when it comes to how to best assist students. Then a third takeaway is the path forward because we have seen from the record of our witnesses today that we really can be phenomenal results when these things are taught in the right way, so Dr. Brumley's State of Louisiana has moved from 50th to 16th in literacy in a relatively short amount of time. In Dr. Harrison's district in Alabama, 257 out of 262 third graders actually met proficiency levels, which is you know, miles ahead of the national average. It is my hope that the work that you have done, and the work that Ms. Roughton's group is doing at schools across California and across the country, as you have shared today, can serve as an inspiration to school leaders and policymakers throughout the country. That young people can learn the skills they need early on to go from learning to read to reading to learn an can learn the foundational math skills that are necessary for the workplace, and to navigate a complex world. If we can do that, then we can reverse our Nation's educational decline. We can help millions of kids have better lives, and our country will be a lot better off for it. Without objection, there being no further business, the Committee stands adjourned. [Whereupon, at 12:01 p.m., the Subcommittee was adjourned.]"
