    "snippet": "HON. MORGAN LUTTRELL, A REPRESENTATIVE IN CONGRESS FROM THE STATE OF TEXAS Mr. Luttrell. Mr. Chairman, Ranking Member, thank you for having me today. You both mentioned the importance of health. I am specifically going to speak on brain health, which I adamantly believe is something that we do not pay attention to, eit...",
    "full_text": "HON. MORGAN LUTTRELL, A REPRESENTATIVE IN CONGRESS FROM THE STATE OF TEXAS Mr. Luttrell. Mr. Chairman, Ranking Member, thank you for having me today. You both mentioned the importance of health. I am specifically going to speak on brain health, which I adamantly believe is something that we do not pay attention to, either in the medical space nationally or globally. As we move through these appropriations, I want to focus on the brain research innovative neurotechnologies through the NIH funding. Back in 2014, this was started. In 2014, I was exiting the military and actually in grad school studying neuroscience, and I did not specifically get to touch that grant funding, but I saw how it started to change the landscape. From 2014 to 2026, what we know about the brain and brain health has changed every second of every minute and every hour of every day. Currently, the 21st Century Cures Act, which provides funding for this program, is sunsetting, and of everything that we do in this wonderful place, one thing that we absolutely need to remember is how important that gelatinous substance between our ears is. It gives us capabilities. It allows us to do amazing things. It allows us--for you to understand what I am saying, contemplate the contemplation of thought, put a man on the moon, everything. If we had the ability to train and educate and cure the diseases of the brain, because we primarily focus from the neck down, and we have been doing that at an exceptional level since we have been walking around. Decreasing funding. I am actually asking for an increase in funding in this program, and this is the reason why. This place always asks for a return on the investment. It does, and if any program has provided or given the American people and the public something, it is this specific program, amongst many others, but this one specifically. It has provided brain mapping because today, currently, we still have no idea how this wonderful organism works. We just do not. We have not figured that out yet, but the scientists and researchers that are in this space have created brain mapping, neural pixels. They have given us the ability for deep brain stimulation. That directly affects Parkinson's disease. I have a family member who had Parkinson's, still does currently. Convulsions and shakes were out of control. Deep brain stimulation paused it. This money directly affects that, and because of the advancements of artificial intelligence, machine learning, and language models that live and breathe in laboratories and institutions of higher education, instead of baby steps, we are taking profound leaps forward. I had a traumatic brain injury when I was in the military, and I spent in the military and my post-military life addressing that issue, and it is the reason I went back to school. Having a pause, purposeful pause in these programs would kill a momentum that we will never get back. My professor said this was the decade of the brain, and that was 10 years ago. These computational analytics are allowing real-time diagnostics. We are able to see things at different levels inside the human brain. I have always been asked, why can we not figure this out? How can this be so challenging if we have been funding and doing these studies for decades? One of the many issues is, sir, your brain is different than mine. Young lady, yours is different than mine. Ma'am, yours is different than mine. You cannot have a control group of a thousand people because every single human brain is different, and you have to see it that way. That is one of the many challenges that our doctors, and clinicians, and scientists, and neuroscientists, and data scientists are facing, but they have got a lane and they are moving at amazing speed. In Washington, DC, I share this House with you. One thing, if we can get rid of something, we can add something. If we can do anything to help the American people, aside of that, if a mother brings her baby into the emergency room with a head injury, the neurologist can look at her and go, ``we have got this, I can give you your child back,'' because, currently, with brain disease and brain injury or brain distractions, we are dead in the water, and this program specifically is breaking through those barricades. Mr. Chairman, Ranking Member, thank you. [The information follows:]"
